        /// <summary>
        /// Initiate a scroll session for retrieving large product datasets.
        /// </summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// Bulk extraction endpoint — full-catalog syncs (PIM, ERP, marketplace), exports,
        /// <br/>search-index reindexing. Also required past the 10,000 offset cap on POST /Query.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>Returns a `scrollId` and the first batch. Pass `scrollId` to
        /// <br/>GET /api/products/Query/Scroll/{scrollId} until it comes back null. Sessions expire after
        /// <br/>10 minutes of inactivity; per-tenant concurrency is capped.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>Same filter set as POST /Query (see request schema) and identical response shape — price
        /// <br/>context filtering, inventory cleanup and property-list shaping apply on both the initial
        /// <br/>batch and continuations. `savedSearchId` works here too (bulk-extract a saved search); the
        /// <br/>saved search is resolved once at initiation, so edits to it never affect an active scroll.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>### Default values are omitted
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>To keep responses small, a field is left out when it holds its default value: `false`, `0`,
        /// <br/>`null`, the empty date `0001-01-01T00:00:00`, the empty id
        /// <br/>`00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000`, or an enum's first value. **A missing field means
        /// <br/>that value, not "unknown".** `""` and `[]` are real values and are always returned.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>A field marked `nullable` in the schema has `null` as its default, so it is left out only
        /// <br/>when it is null — a nullable boolean holding `false`, or a nullable number holding `0`, is
        /// <br/>still returned.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>For full bulk-sync recipes (delta queries, idempotent pipelines) and response-size
        /// <br/>guidance, see https://docs.omnium.no/docs/Integrations/product-query.
        /// <br/>            
        /// <br/>### Canonical example
        /// <br/>Delta sync — capture every product touched in a 5-minute window (modifications, publish/
        /// <br/>unpublish events, price activations/deactivations) in a single pass. Run on a cron for an
        /// <br/>idempotent replication pipeline:
        /// <br/>```json
        /// <br/>{
        /// <br/>  "marketIds": ["NOR"],
        /// <br/>  "scrollSize": 500,
        /// <br/>  "isUnionDeltaQuery": true,
        /// <br/>  "modifiedFrom": "2025-01-08T10:15:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "startPublishFrom": "2025-01-08T10:15:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "startPublishTo": "2025-01-08T10:20:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "stopPublishFrom": "2025-01-08T10:15:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "stopPublishTo": "2025-01-08T10:20:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "priceActivatedFrom": "2025-01-08T10:15:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "priceActivatedTo": "2025-01-08T10:20:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "priceDeactivatedFrom": "2025-01-08T10:15:00Z",
        /// <br/>  "priceDeactivatedTo": "2025-01-08T10:20:00Z"
        /// <br/>}
        /// <br/>```
        /// </remarks>
        /// <param name="body">Query request with filters, market/store context, and field selection</param>
        /// <returns>Scroll initiated successfully</returns>
        /// <exception cref="OmniumApiException">A server side error occurred.</exception>
        public virtual System.Threading.Tasks.Task<SwaggerResponse<OmniumProductOmniumScrollableQueryResult>> ProductQueryQueryScrollAsync(OmniumProductScrollQueryRequest? body)
        {
            return ProductQueryQueryScrollAsync(body, System.Threading.CancellationToken.None);
        }

